 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Where it's decided:** `src/tessel/wrappers.py:60-86`, the `Request.max_content_length` property resolves in this order:\n1. `self._max_content_length`, if it was set on the request (per-request override).\n2. Without an app context, Werkzeug's default (`None`).\n3. Otherwise `active_app.config[\"MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH\"]`, the global config.\n\n**Per request:** Yes. There's a setter (`wrappers.py:88-90`), and the changelog and docs describe it as per-request customizable. Set it in the view before touching `request.files`, `request.form`, or `request.get_data()`, since the limit is enforced when the body is read:\n\n```python\n@app.post(\"/upload\")\ndef upload():\n    request.max_content_length = 100 * 1024 * 1024  # 100 MB, this request only\n    f = request.files[\"file\"]\n    ...\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The global `MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH` stays as is for every other route.\n- If form parsing also hits a limit, `max_form_memory_size` and `max_form_parts` can be overridden the same way.\n- If you'd rather not touch the view body, set it in a `before_request` handler that checks `request.endpoint`.",
